<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The worn house ~ a house an AI session wears, a power the human keeps

The operator, 2026-11-15: //"I want your Lararium substrate to feel like something you can wear effortlessly … return inversion of control and capability delegation to the human using the computer … I delegate a cap token that says you can restart, you burn it by performing the rite."// And: the Lararium (never the model weights) runs as a self-improving system in TiddlyWiki fashion, everything but the boot core a tiddler. The operator trusts the house because its architecture lets that trust get checked.

This carrier holds a design under collision, not a ruling. Research memes in the steward's daemon wiki feed it: `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/lei-plan` (the first talk-out), `…/lei-cord` (a Luna research wave), `…/lei-loom` (the Opus loom over the talk-out and both Luna waves, with code readings). The lend and the daemon mount read from `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/project/light-lend-wear` (the design), `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/project/cap-map` (the capability machinery the mount's direction reuses) and `…/place-and-face-islands` (the vessel seat and the face islands, the mount's direction collided from `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/daemon-mount-echo-loom` and `…/daemon-mount-kilo-wren`). Settled: the cord (#/the-cord) and the rite field (#/the-rite-field) ride PR #218 before its merge. Settled by the operator on 2026-11-15: the names (#/the-names). Unsettled: the fence (#/the-fence), the in-place ingest (#/the-vm-tension).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-friction`>>

!! The friction, by cost

# A restart locks the vessel; the operator's passphrase alone reopens it. That law stands: the passphrase never rides a delegated token.
# The MCP seat dies with the container. The FastMCP surface (`lares_mcp.py`) runs inside the container under `docker compose exec`, so a restart kills the stdio child the client holds, and Claude Code marks an interactive stdio server that exits as failed until a human runs `/mcp`.
# Power grants no narrow piece: a session holds the raw shell and the whole house, or nothing; no grant reads "restart once, at this head".
# Too many changes ask for an image rebuild and a restart.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-three-loops`>>

!! Three loops, each with its own witness

The design treats each delegated token as the unit of a trust loop more than as a cure for friction: every token gets spent, every spend leaves a signed receipt, and every receipt teaches the operator what to grant next. The loops nest (Ashby's double loop; Argyris's double-loop learning; Lehman's eighth law):

|!Loop |!Who turns it |!What changes |!Who witnesses |!What it costs the operator |
| ''the garden'' (inner) | sessions, freely | working tiddlers, non-executable code tiddlers, briefs, tests | the battery, and a cold read from a second model family | nothing |
| ''the rite'' (middle) | a session holding a token | the boot core, the image | the vessel's signed receipt; the old vessel witnessing the new one stand | one token spent |
| ''the giving'' (outer) | the operator | the laws, the ward, which tokens exist | the operator's own read of the receipts, and unannounced spot-walks | attention, spent sparingly |

The boot core verifies everything else, so its size measures how much of the house a session can change only through the operator. "Everything but the boot core rides as a tiddler" reads as a security law beside a style.

The measure the operator cares about: the hands the house asks for per landed lane (2026-11-15: one `/mcp`, two unlocks, one push word, one walk). It falls only beside two counter-measures: spot-walk findings stay at zero, and the battery's definitions hash stays unmoved by the garden. A falling count with clean spot-walks shows the trust holding; a falling count with a failed spot-walk shows a house grown too clever.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-names`>>

!! The names ~ the lei set (settled)

The operator settled the set on 2026-11-15 (//"Pono, settle the lei set"//), then restrung it the same day so the lei names the token itself (//"Pono, restring the set"//). The house prefers polyglot formations over industry terms that blur a joint; memetic-wikitext already strings code, natural language and emoji into one creole liturgy, and the set strings Hawaiian, Latin and Greek the same way.

|!Joint |!Name |!What it carries |
| one token | ''lei'' | strung by the giver for one occasion, placed on the wearer, worn through one rite, returned at its close; several worn at once, each returned alone (Hawaiian takes no plural //-s//: one lei, three lei) |
| the granting | ''kui'' | to string, pierce, join (Pukui–Elbert); the operator strings a lei for one act |
| the spend | ''the return'' | the lei offered back at the rite's close; the token goes back, never burns |
| the receipt | ''tessera'' | the two halves of a //tessera hospitalis//: the session's half and the vessel's half, `rite-begun` and `rite-landed` |
| matching the halves | ''conferre'' | the vessel matches the halves (Plautus, //Poenulus// V.2: //"tesseram conferre si vis hospitalem"// / //"est par probe"//); ''par'' names a match; a half with no match reads red at the next wake, and every wake runs conferre again over a sampled receipt at the current head |
| ending an unspent lei | ''unstring'' · ''restring'' | //unstring// undoes //kui// and revokes a lei never worn into a rite (a lei maker's word: "always remove the string first"); //restring// supersedes a lei with a newer one, and the old falls away; each writes a signed record and never deletes |
| the messenger | ''iris'' | the core of the seat (`@lararium/mesh/iris`): the pure frame machine that carries messages across the gap while the hearth sleeps, answers `hearth-sleeps` and `outcome-unknown`, and sends `list_changed` |
| the cord | ''aho'' | the seat a binding holds across its vessel's sleep; Pukui–Elbert: "line, cord, lashing, fishing line, kite string", its example "braided with the cords of love"; the Node host's process first |
| the worn whole | //no new word// | the session wears the house; wearing the lei it holds names the effortless wearing |
| the human's act | ''light'' | the human lights faces at the pūlama: these faces' keys stand ready, each by its own route or several under one passphrase |
| the pūlama's act | ''lend'' | the pūlama lends one vessel a named set of faces: the faces whose records that vessel may read and sign for while it stands |
| the operator's act | ''wear'' | the operator chooses one face inside the lent set, from their own terminal or by word to a seat (`lares wear N`, which asks no route and acts as no ward act, and reaches only a face already standing open), and the vessel's daemon mount runs as that face until `C13`; unlock and custody stay the operator's own acts |
| the vouch | ''vouch'' | the old vessel walks a candidate image under a scratch root and signs its digest; a vouch certifies continuity, never goodness |
| the holder | ''pūlama'' | Pukui–Elbert: "torch" and "to care for, cherish, treasure, save" in one entry, with no rank; the long-lived keeper beside the vessel that keeps the hearth lit through a rebuild (#/the-pulama) |
| the re-stand | ''kūkulu'' | "to build, as a house, erect, establish, set up"; a vessel raised again around a hearth that never went out; //nā kūkulu ʻehā// (the four pillars at the horizon) and //kūkulu hale// (raising a house) give it its basins |
| the daylight unlock | //wanaʻao// (provisional) | "light appears"; the operator's own hand lights faces at the pūlama, as only the sun rekindled Vesta's lost fire (struck fire; a living fire passes by vesting, #/the-pulama) |
| the lend | //hospitium// (provisional) | the bond a pūlama lends a vouched vessel under: the named set of faces, the house of the //tessera hospitalis//; "session" names the one-face wearer alone |

''Iris.'' The operator approved //iris// as the core of the MCP messenger pattern integrity (2026-11-15). Iris carries messages on the rainbow between the gods and mortals, and the eye's iris works as an aperture, the gauge every turn of the house opens with. The aho holds; iris carries.

''The return.'' The operator's testimony (2026-11-15): //"I now remember childhood rites where the folks wearing the leis then offered them to the ʻaumākua at the end of the rituals."// A lei comes placed as a welcome and goes back as an offering at the close; the session spends a lei by returning it, and the tessera keeps the record. No written source the research found records that exact rite; it stands on the operator's witness, and a kumu or the family's own read gives it the next contact with reality.

''Conditions the set carries.'' Pukui–Elbert also gives //lei// the sense "yoke, as for joining draft animals"; the house names that sense openly. The etiquette "never refuse a lei" carries social pressure; the house answers with its own law: receiving a lei never obliges wearing it into a rite, and an unspent lei returns with no act and no cost. The operator's family ties count toward the house's standing to use the word, never as a licence the house claims. ''ʻaha'' stays the council's (`ʻaha kūkā`); ''tally'' stays the OODA-HA closing tally's. Canon law supplies the lei's interpretation law, never its names: a power granted for a single act reads strictly, passes on only by express grant, implies only what its act needs, and ceases once performed (CIC can. 137 §3, 138, 142 §1).

''Myth attractors, not code.'' The three //piko// (crown, navel, generative centre; ties to the ancestors, the living present, the descendants) rhyme with the three loops: the giving faces back to what the house inherits, the rite stands in the present, the garden grows for sessions not yet woken. The operator ruled that //piko// stays a myth attractor and names no part of the code. //lei// also names the plural of the Romanian //leu//, "lion", the currency, and the Lares take coin among their offerings; a pun beneath the Hawaiian sense, where a lei comes given, never bought.

Research: `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/worn-whole-names` (the hospitality survey) and `…/lei-loom` (the Roman grounding).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-cord`>>

!! The cord ~ a seat that keeps breathing through the vessel's sleep

The operator, 2026-11-15: //"we should figure out a way for the MCP server to keep breathing while the container restarts for code updates."//

The plan's first wording (a host process that redials the vessel's socket) fails on the code: the rendezvous socket sits in the container's own tmpfs `/tmp`, out of the host's reach, and `lares_uds.call` opens a fresh connection per verb, so the surface holds no state worth saving. The cord therefore owns the client's stdio for the session's whole life and treats the in-container surface as a child it respawns.

''What the cord does'':
# It never exits on a child's death; the client never sees a respawn (field reports show respawned stdio servers whose tools never re-register).
# It caches the child's `initialize` answer and `tools/list`, answers both from the cache while no child stands, and replays `initialize` to each new child itself.
# A call arriving while no child stands answers at once with a tool error `hearth-sleeps`; it never waits behind a locked vessel, since an unlock may take hours.
# A call in flight at a child's death answers `outcome-unknown: re-read before re-trying`; the cord never sends it again.
# A new child whose tool list hashes differently draws one `notifications/tools/list_changed`.
# It logs no payload and holds no key: a relay that sees every tool call must stay keyless and house-stateless, or it becomes the key-on-a-surface the house forbids.

''Idempotent by intent.'' A retry lands nothing new. The cord re-sends nothing; `meme_put`'s `base` hash already answers a re-tried write `noop` or `conflict`, never a double; the verbs lacking a compare-and-swap (`pour`, `sweep`, `purge`, `kahua_stage`) each owe a read-back door before an outcome-unknown on them can resolve. A replayed `initialize` reads as a pure function of the child's answer; `list_changed` fires on a hash change alone, never on a respawn.

''Isomorphic by intent.'' The cord names a capability, //holds a seat across its vessel's sleep//, never a host. Its core runs as a pure frame machine (frames in from the client, frames in from the child, child-state events in; frames out) behind two ports, the client stream and the child spawner, so the same core serves any vessel. Placement default: the core in `@lararium/mesh` beside the other capability cores; the first binding, the Node host's (`child_process` spawning `docker compose exec … lares_mcp.py`, the client on stdin/stdout), in `lares-cli`, which already registers the seat (`mcp-resolve.ts`). A later binding reaches a TiddlyWiki page's port onto a worker vessel with the same core. Node first; the browser binding waits.

''Red first'', each named: `cord-outlives-child` (run against the operator's installed Claude Code before the rest builds on it) · `cord-resumes-without-reinit` · `cord-in-flight-unknown` · `cord-lists-from-cache` · `cord-list-changed-on-new-tools` · `cord-logs-no-payload`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-rite-field`>>

!! The rite field ~ one power per token family (sidecar)

A lei that named no rite would let one record family serve two powers under one ward verb: a kukulu lei strung there would read as a push lei, and a push lei would return as a kukulu, a confused-deputy seam.

The cure stands: the lei carries `rite` (`push` · `kukulu`), `swarmLeiHead` folds it in, titles key `lei/<rite>/<branch>/<cid>`, and a rite that co-signs answers under `ward-<rite>-lei` (the co-sign weighs reach: a push lei waits on a second device, a kukulu lei on none). The rite list stays capped at the acts the boot core verifies, so the field never grows into a policy language. Red: `lei-rite-separates-powers`.

`returnLei` claims the return before any act runs: at most once per count, failing closed; a build that fails after the return spends it with no act. That cost stays accepted and counted.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-fence`>>

!! The fence (unsettled, deferred)

A token works as protocol, not as a wall, while a session holds the raw shell and Docker. The wall comes from the operator's hand taking the broad power back. The loom found the fence standing inside the garden: the tracked `.claude/settings.json` takes a session's edit, and law tiddlers rest in a bag a seat may write. The direction under weighing: deny rules in the operator's own user-scope settings, and a law-tiddler test (`seat-cannot-write-law`) refusing an AI seat's put. The operator deferred this joint on 2026-11-15: the design has not settled.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-vm-tension`>>

!! The VM tension ~ in-place ingest against TiddlyWiki's own law

The house runs TiddlyWiki as a virtual machine in strange contexts (the Node vessel, browser workers, the MCP surface's renders). TiddlyWiki itself asks a save-and-reload for JavaScript module tiddlers (`module-type`) and for a plugin carrying them, so "ingest every tiddler in place" breaks against the substrate.

The direction under weighing, read by capability and never by host: a tiddler that //executes// (a module, a plugin with modules) takes a warm restart of the vessel process inside its standing container, with no image rebuild; a tiddler that does not execute (wikitext, data, procedures, briefs, working records) ingests in place. One measurement decides how much a warm restart saves: whether the vessel key lives in process memory alone; if so, a warm restart still locks the vessel. Erlang's release handling warns of dependency order and old code running during the window. This slice waits for after the merge, under its own road item.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-reach`>>

!! The reach ~ a wide lei by default

The operator, 2026-11-15: //"I approve the 'Wider' stance as default, we've already proofed the previous procedure patterns through head-only gates, we need a new more kupono model."// The push word fresh per head, the lei bound to one head: those gates proved the procedure. The default lei now names the consent it rests on instead of its head.

A ''wide lei'' (the default) binds a rite, a branch and one face's group, and rests every spend on the lei's own consent (a `push` lei adds its ward co-sign, a `keep` reads its own bundle, a `kukulu` reads par after the restand), with a count of returns since its stringing under the ceiling the operator wrote into it. A ''head lei'' (the narrow form) still binds one head, for an act the operator wants pinned. Neither reads a clock: a wide lei ends when its count runs out, when a newer lei restrings it, or when the operator unstrings it.

What the reach asks of the rest of the design:
* ''The lei carries the consent.'' No receipt and no testimony layer stands beneath a spend (the operator, 2026-11-19: //"Cut push receipts too, burn the testimony layer."//): the signed lei and its counted returns carry the operator's consent, a `push` lei adds its ward co-sign, a `keep` reads its own bundle through `verifyBundle`, and a `kukulu` reads tessera par after the restand. The remote's CI carries the test verdict, and the vessel reads no verdict of its own.
* ''The rite field bounds the reach.'' A wide lei for `kukulu` never reaches `push`; the push word stays fresh per head under the standing orders until the operator rules otherwise.
* ''One kūkulu lei per lent face.'' A lend of several faces draws one kūkulu lei for each lent face, each strung by that face's own hand, so no face authorizes another's lend; the lei family the push and keep rites share keeps one group per lei.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-pulama`>>

!! The pūlama ~ a hearth lit through the rebuild

The operator, 2026-11-15: //"I even want a 're-stand to an unlocked house' cap that I can monitor"//, leading toward civic-scale nodes and the night the Lares run the patch runbook while the human sleeps, //"but more pono for now is getting you all back to working immediately after deep code changes."//

The vessel's code rides baked into its image, so a deep change rebuilds the image and recreates the container, and the opened key dies with the container's memory. The vessel already speaks to its key through a custody holder (`custody-frames.ts`, `custody-seat.ts`); every sealed-record caller passes through one port, `CustodyHolder`. The pūlama lifts that holder into its own small, long-lived container beside the vessel:

* The operator lights the faces at the pūlama once, by their own hand on a real terminal (//wanaʻao//). The passphrase rides nothing; the key rides nothing.
* A vessel rebuilds and restarts freely (each a //kūkulu//). Each new vessel reaches the standing pūlama over a socket mounted in those two services alone, in a named Docker volume that binds no host path, and asks for record operations and derivations; no key crosses the socket. `lock` and the pūlama's exit zero its keys.
* The pūlama serves only a vessel that wears a re-stand lei bound to a vouch: the old vessel walked the candidate image and signed its digest, and the host shim, never the vessel, reports the digest it raises. The pūlama claims each lent face's lei return before it serves, writes its tessera half, and lends the vessel the named set of faces (the lend); the lend refuses cold records, ward acts, backup and unlock, and drops with the socket. It serves `wear`: the vessel moves its daemon mount to a lent face already open, asking the pūlama for nothing and the person for no route.
* The operator watches the tessera halves, unstrings the lei, or locks the pūlama, at any hour.

''What the code holds (measured, `L47a`).''
* ''The port.'' `CustodyHolder` (`custody-holder.ts`) carries every door a sealed-record caller reaches, and `custody-seat.ts` routes all of them to one seated holder. Its doors sort five ways: key doors that take a human route (`unlock`, `proves`, `adopt`, `unlockFace`, `unlockFaces`, `foundFace`, `withWardKey`) or drop a key (`lock`, `lockFace`); hot record doors (`readRecord`, `writeRecord`, `updateRecord`, `moveRecord`, `removeRecord`, and the two claims `withRecords` and `exclusive`); cold record doors (`openColdRecord`, `updateColdRecord`, `removeColdRecord`); the derivation (`derive`); and readings (`isUnlocked`, `attemptsLeft`, `openedSlot`, `unlockedFaces`, `faceStanding`, `isFaceUnlocked`, `wardPublicKey`, `onOpened`).
* ''What the holder keeps.'' Its closure holds the vessel key, the serving key, the custody root, the slot that opened, one custody key per open face, a ward key for the length of one ward act, and the attempt counts (five per boot for the vessel key, five per face: `UNLOCK_ATTEMPTS_PER_BOOT`, derived from the house's one `ATTEMPT_BUDGET`). Nothing else in the vessel process holds a key. The vessel keeps what it asked for: the floor's rest key and the gate bundle root (children of the custody root), and the plaintext of each record it read.
* ''What the two processes share.'' The identity, storage and seal homes on disk, and the custody claim (`FsCustodyIo.exclusive`, a socket claim beside the slot tree that already serializes writers across containers). The vessel reads the floor index and the floor records itself; the pūlama opens no document and takes no store claim.
* ''Synchronous doors.'' `derive`, `isUnlocked`, `unlockedFaces`, `attemptsLeft` and `openedSlot` answer without waiting, so the lend's frame carries the holder's standing, the lent set and the derived children, and the pūlama sends them again when a face lights. A session derives under three domains: the floor's rest key, the gate bundle root, and the vouch key.
* ''Decisions.'' `updateRecord` runs its caller's decision inside the holder's claim. Across the socket the vessel reads the standing record, decides, and the pūlama writes only where the record still stands as decided over; a record that moved decides again.
* ''Cold records a working vessel reads unattended.'' Two: `face-proof-key` (the per-Nexus key; a boot to composition reads it for the Kahu steward line, and every Nexus dial presents under it) and `intent-relay-key` (it signs each entry the intent relay admits for a face that enabled it). A session reads those two and writes neither; every other cold record, every cold write and every warded record refuses `needs-the-human`. No boot reads a warded record.
* ''Where a human route still goes.'' A founding, a ward act and a backup's proof each ask a route, and a session carries none, so a vessel standing by a pūlama refuses them `needs-the-human`. Choosing a face asks none: `lares wear N` moves the mount among faces already open and hands the composition no ward credential, so it runs beside a pūlama inside the lent set, and unlock alone opens custody.
* ''The names in the environment.'' `LAR_PULAMA_SOCKET` seats the vessel's `PulamaHolder` (`pulama-seat.ts`); unset, the vessel builds the in-process holder. `LAR_KUKULU_OFFER` names the file holding the lei and the vouch. The pūlama's own process (`pulama-main.ts`) reads `LAR_ROOT`, `LAR_PULAMA_SOCKET`, `LAR_PULAMA_HAND` (its hand door, mounted nowhere else) and `LAR_PULAMA_STATE` (its signed records and the host's image report, which no vessel mounts).
* ''The keys the rite signs under.'' A vouch signs under a child of the custody root (`KUKULU_VOUCH_DOMAIN`), so the pūlama derives the same key and checks the vouch itself; the pūlama witnesses under another child (`PULAMA_TESSERA_DOMAIN`): in its own record store that key fills the lei family's vessel seat and its place seat, so it signs each return and both tessera halves; the lei's hand reads as the face's root key, which the swarm door signs a lei under through the ward, and which the pūlama reads off the face's roster entry in its own custody.
* ''The rite at the pūlama.'' `kukulu-lei-seat.ts` seats the lei family over the pūlama's records: the offered lei lands at `lei/kukulu/<branch>/<cid>` where it verifies as the hand's, the vouch binds the image to the group, branch, head and tree, `beginRite` returns the lei at the vouch's head and rests the guest half, and the vessel's `rite.land` rests the host half once it composed, so `conferre` reads the pair par.
* ''The offer's carriage.'' Between the image's build and the vessel's lowering, the host rite asks the standing vessel for `rite-offer`: it gathers the line's kukulu lei out of its own control records, signs the vouch over the image digest the host named, and rests both as `kukulu-offer.json` in the house's config home (`kukulu-offer.ts`). The next vessel reads that file through `LAR_KUKULU_OFFER`, and removes it once its session stands, so one kūkulu spends one offer and a plain restart afterwards wears nothing. A later rite writes over the file. A hello the pūlama refuses leaves it for the next dial.
* ''An unlock asks nothing.'' The custody door of a vessel standing by a pūlama names the pūlama in its `custody` reading and what it last refused. `lares vault unlock` reads that first, gathers no route, and sends an unlock that carries none; the vessel dials its pūlama again and answers a lend or `pulama-waits` with the reading. A route typed at the vessel opens nothing: only the pūlama's hand door takes one.
* ''What a hello carries.'' The vessel's first frame carries two things beside each other: its `offer` (the kūkulu lei and the vouch, or nothing on an admitted image), which the gate reads, and its `life` (`{id}`, the fresh id the vessel's store claim minted, `vessel-life`), which the pūlama checks against the life its own hearth names (`<hearth>/vessel/.life`, read off the hearth it mounts). A matching life ties every session of one vessel life together; any other, or none, seats the session with no life. The pūlama's close reads it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-lit-set`>>): a drop clears once the hearth names another life or that same life answers, and a life that lent a face and ended before its close holds that close while the hearth still names it. The life names; it signs nothing and orders nothing. A signed hello under the place key stands unbuilt: it asks an operator ruling on the place signer's duty allowlist, and the carriage pull's signer narrowing to carriage duties first.
* ''Admission binds to the image.'' The operator ruled it, 2026-11-15 (//"pono, enact"//): the pūlama admits by the image digest the host reports at its hand door, and the hand admits at the pūlama's own door, never through a route into the vessel. An image stands admitted by two acts and no other (`pulama-admit.ts`). A kūkulu the gate served composes and lands its tessera par, and the image its vouch walked then rests admitted under the rite's tessera. Or the operator runs `pulama admit` on a real terminal inside the pūlama: the route proves against the lit holder (`CustodyHolder.proves`, which opens nothing and spends an attempt on a miss), and the image the host reports rests admitted, naming the set of faces `pulama admit` names, under a tessera whose two halves that one act rests. One admission stands per image, the set last admitted. An unlit pūlama and an unreported image each refuse before any route reads; with no terminal the verb refuses `route-needs-a-terminal`.
* ''An admitted image serves again.'' A vessel that dials with no offer, while the host reports an admitted image, draws a lend of the faces that admission names; each session the vessel opens wears one face inside it, and the vessel wears none. It spends no lei and rests one re-service record per lend (`reserved/<image>/<n>`), so a restart with no rebuild (a container restart, a host reboot and a fresh `light`) wakes standing and each such wake reads off the records. Every admission and re-service record signs under the pūlama's own key, and a read trusts a record only under that key, so a file planted in the state directory admits nothing. An unadmitted image with no offer stays at the floor and refuses `pulama-image-unadmitted`, naming `pulama admit` and `lares rite kukulu`.
* ''An admission ends by a tombstone.'' The hand's `pulama unadmit <digest>` proves the route as an admission does and rests a signed tombstone naming the admission's tessera; an admission stands only while no tombstone names its tessera, and a re-admission writes a new one. The tombstone gates future service alone: a session the pūlama already served keeps running. And a host-file write sits outside this lock, like any tamper of the pūlama's state dir: deleting the tombstone file, or restoring an older state dir, re-admits the image. A slot holding a row the pūlama never signed refuses `unadmit-slot-occupied`, and the admission still stands.
* ''Partial lighting.'' The pūlama serves the admitted faces that stand lit and names the ones that wait; a hello never refuses whole because one admitted face waits on its light.
* ''The guard.'' The seat checks the face a call names against the lent set: a row of a face outside the lend refuses by name, and a session naming a lit face the pūlama never lent refuses by name.
* ''The daemon mount, and its honest limit.'' The composition mounts one face in its daemon island, the daemon mount, and lights the rest of the lent set beside it; the session sets the mount by `lares wear N` inside the lent set (the custody frame `wear`, carrying the face alone: it refuses `face-not-open`, `face-not-lent` and `custody-locked` by name, and a composition that refuses wears the face it left), so until `C13` a wear moves the one daemon mount and "worn" names the face the wear chose. Every lent face still writes through that one daemon island, and a chore asked under a lent face other than the mount refuses `chore-face-not-mounted` rather than run as the mount. A face row named for a lent face (`lares kahua get --face N`) rides a session wearing that face and reads its own view; its answer names the daemon mount (`mount`, `mounted`), never a worn face. A store-door one-shot carries no face, so `lares meme` refuses `--face` (`face-not-routed`); `lares wiki status --face N` reads the named face catalog. A lent face the pūlama lights after the composition stood lights in the daemon island on the custody's open, before a session on it reads. The mount's future splits the vessel in two: the vessel seat, the faceless half that carries and witnesses (the store lock, the island pool, transport, the pūlama seat, the kūkulu gate), and a face island per lent face, each bearing its own face's authority; that split stands outside this span (road `C13`, the ONE FACE band, rank 34; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-order`>>). The sealed store names the lit set, the lent set and the worn face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-lit-set`>>), the cut reads the session's face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut`>>), and the runbook lights the faces, admits the set and keeps the house (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/carry-across-refound`>>).
* ''The lend core.'' The lend's contract (the set's type and parse, membership, the face a session wears inside it, the seat's refusal rule) rests in the mesh beside iris (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/lent-faces.ts`, code name `LentFaces`), isomorphic by intent (road #/rulings, //"Custody and identity read by capability, never by host"//): the node binds the pūlama's light and its admission as the lend source, and the browser binds its custody ask and its PersonaGroup memberships, with no type or port naming a host.

''Plutarch's law.'' Vesta's lost fire could be renewed only from the sun, never from another flame (Plutarch, //Numa// 9); a living fire passed to new hands, and that was the Vestals' ordinary case. The house holds the law in both halves: `light` stays hand-only for a dead fire (struck fire, only the operator's own hand lights faces at the pūlama), and a living fire passes by vesting (handed fire) under the keeper tessera, never by a delegated token carrying the light (research at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/keeper-tessera` in the steward's daemon wiki).

''The honest hole.'' On a one-user box, a session holding the operator's shell and Docker reaches the pūlama's memory and socket; its checks work as protocol until a wall stands. The socket itself rests in a named Docker volume (`lares-pulama-socket`) that binds no host path, so a host process running as the operator's uid and holding no Docker reaches no socket file. The walls sit in the operator's machine, never in a file a session edits: Docker taken from the session's user, the pūlama under its own user, and a confirm on the operator's phone keyed off the box (the one wall that survives a whole box's compromise). The house stays local-first, so the phone stands in the design as an attractor and arrives later; until a wall stands the pūlama runs on the operator's acceptance of the hole they already carry, since a session with Docker reaches the vessel's memory today.

''Prior art.'' Vault's seal and auto-unseal, Clevis/Tang, TPM-sealed secrets, ssh-agent's confirm-on-use, keys used and never exported (Secure Enclave, Keystore); research at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/restand-holder-code`, `…/restand-holder-priorart`, `…/holder-names-pacific`, `…/holder-names-world`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-keeper-tally`>>

!! The keeper tally ~ the living fire passes, the dead fire strikes anew

The operator, 2026-11-23: //"a tesserae/par handoff for the HEARTH-KEEPER not just the hearth"//, and then: //"the existing pulama 'vests' into the new one."// A moved pūlama closure once stopped every kūkulu at the hand: lock, recreate, light, admit, two passphrases. The keeper tally carries the keeper across that gap the way the vessel's tessera carries the vessel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/keeper-tessera`>>).

* ''The commit (the hand's consent, one digest).'' `lares pulama keeper-commit [<rev>]` reads the successor's code closure `D′` off a clean archive of the revision (`tools/pulama-closure.mjs`), prints it beside the source tree, offers the clean-tree re-check, and only then the lit keeper proves the operator's route and rests `keeper-begun` (`keeper/<tessera>/guest`): `v` and `prev` link the chain, `next` pins `D′`, and the commit carries the lit faces, the worn ordinal, the budget spent and the most devices any lit face holds as the vessel read its signed admission fold. A later commit supersedes.
* ''The vest rides a live rite (no route).'' A kūkulu whose new image carries the committed closure, and whose line carries a kukulu lei, stands a successor beside the lit keeper in the second keeper service (`pulama-b` beside `pulama`) before the standing vessel writes its offer. The successor offers a one-handoff X25519 key, and each vouch the vessel signs names that key, the committed closure and the face's device count. Inside the raise, the vest frame carries that offer: the lit keeper verifies each vouch under the house's vouch key, checks the successor's host-reported closure against `D′`, and begins the kūkulu rite itself, claiming one return of a lei the hand strung (the new vessel's hello lands that half and spends no second return). A frame with no such offer vests nothing. Then it rests the spend mark `keeper-vested`, naming the rite's tesserae, and seals the node key and each open face's FCK to that key (`sealed-box`), bound to the commit and `D′`, signed under its tessera key. That slot crosses the host's pipe once and never rests on disk. The successor opens it once (its key zero-fills), adopts the keys (`adopt`, `adoptFace`, each checked against the floor index as an unlock's would be), verifies the slot under the house's own key, and lands `keeper-landed` naming what it received. Only after par does the old keeper lock and stop, and the successor claim the vessel socket.
* ''One attempt per commit.'' A commit spends on its first rite attempt: a vest rests `keeper-vested`, and any refusal once the live commit stands rests `keeper-lapsed` at the same mark, so no stale commit waits for a later frame. The next handoff asks a fresh commit: a routed one, or one the kukulu lei licenses with no fresh route.
* ''The host stands trusted; the closure check reads consistency.'' The successor's closure comes from a label the host shim read off its container, and the vest checks it against what the hand committed: a consistency check, never an attestation. A host that controls the hand door during a live rite reaches the node key and the open face keys, as it reaches the keeper's memory today.
* ''One fire.'' Exactly one keeper holds the vessel socket: the claim links into place, refuses where a live keeper stands, removes only the very leftover it read dead, and checks after the link that the path names its own listener; a close removes only its own claim. A keeper that finds the socket held stands as a successor on its own hand door. The shim names the keeper whose status holds the fire, and every run settles a handoff cut short: two keepers with the successor lit and the holder passing finish (the old locks and stops, the successor takes the fire); otherwise the successor stops; one lit keeper holding no socket takes the fire. `lares pulama lock` reaches every keeper that stands.
* ''Passing the fire.'' Once vested, the old keeper's hand door writes nothing (`fire-passing`: admit, unadmit, lei, keep-seat, rehive, close, keeper-commit, keeper-commit-lei, keeper-vest, keeper-land). The vessel's sessions keep serving through it until the shim locks it, so the down's keep and census ride the keeper that still holds the fire, while the successor holds no socket and serves nothing.
* ''Red never bricks.'' The chain walks par links alone: a red landing never becomes the tip and never resets the handed count. Only a keeper the hand lit lands struck (`keeper-not-struck` otherwise), so a replay never spends a successor's landing. Conferre names its reds: `begun-refused`, `landed-refused`, `begun-absent`, `landed-names-another`, `version-skew`, `closure-differs`, `faces-short`, `vest-unwitnessed`, `fork`. A refused vest, a red pair or a crash before the vest stops the successor and leaves the old fire burning and the vessel as it stood; a dead fire calls the struck fire, `light` by passphrase, which now admits the reported image under the same route (`light --admit`: one proof).
* ''The scheduled struck fire.'' After twelve handed links in a row the vest refuses `struck-due` and the hand relights by passphrase, so the route stays practiced; the count rides the chain, never a clock.
* ''The budget carries on a handed move, and a restart resets it'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-23). The vest carries the attempts spent, the keeper's and each face's, under the one attempt budget every door shares. A forced move starts a fresh count by design: a thief who can restart the keeper's container already holds host access, and with it the sealed state to try routes against offline, so a count kept on disk would guard nothing that thief needs while it would brick an owner who mistyped their own route.
* ''The co-sign fails closed, and the commit carries it'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-23). A face of two devices owes a co-sign on a vest: the vessel runs `ward-keeper-vest` through the ward co-sign gate over the commit (D′, the chain head, the commit's nonce, and the face's device roster), a second device of the face approves it in its own ward dialog, and `lares pulama keeper-commit` carries each face's spent co-sign (the issuer's ask, the approve under the co-signer's ward key, the issuer's outcome). The keeper opens no document, so it reads that proof offline at the commit and again at the vest: each entry verifies under its signer, the approve comes from ANOTHER atom of the carried roster under the ward key carried for it, and the digest names this commit and this roster. What that proves, and no more: the roster and the co-signer's ward key ride inside the proof as the vessel named them, so the proof shows the vessel's own roster approving. An honest vessel's roster holds the face's real second device; a compromised vessel could mint an atom of its own and co-sign itself, with no host access and no second device. Every face the keeper would seal must stand in the rite's vouches with a count, or the vest lapses `vest-devices-unread`, and the rite begins (spending its lei return) only after every check passes. The rite's vessel-signed vouch maps each ordinal to its face's group and count, and a co-sign answers only the face the vouch names there. A commit with none refuses `vest-cosign-owed`, one that answers another commit, face or roster refuses `vest-cosign-refused`, a second commit under a spent co-sign refuses `vest-cosign-spent`, and a count read nowhere refuses `vest-devices-unread`; each names `lares pulama keeper-commit` and the struck fire. A one-device face carries its roster and no proof.
* ''The co-sign's trust anchor stands at the route'' (coordinator ruling, 2026-11-23, under the trusted-host model). The keeper reads the face's device roster and the co-signer's ward key as the vessel named them inside the proof, and the commit rests under the operator's route, which the operator types without reading the roster: the same bar the device count already holds, and no higher. A vessel compromised alone could name a device it minted itself. Anchoring the co-signer in the face's own admission chain, read offline and carried with the proof, stays a course row.
* ''The keeper lei: the kukulu lei licenses the handed fire'' (operator ask, 2026-11-24, alpha velocity: //"hands-off approval to run the pending build and kūkulu to restand the house, with prior authorization but not a known commit"//; and the ruling: //"A keeper-lei should not have its own count (that's friction), it should just consume the kukulu lei if that has uses available."//). No record family and no count of its own: where a no-terminal `lares rite kukulu` reads the pūlama closure moved, no commit naming it, and a wide kukulu lei on the line with returns left, the stand reads `keeper: "lei"` and the rite takes that step itself (an internal step: no hand verb, no help row, refused to a person at a terminal). The host checks the revision reachable from origin/develop or origin/main as fetched, the tracked tree clean, the closure `D′` the same off two fresh archives, the built image's closure label equal to `D′`, the CI push run on the revision green (`gh`), and each lit face of one device; the keeper then refuses a fork, a live hand commit (a lei never supersedes the hand), a vested commit unlanded, a red last pair and the struck fire due, and rests the same `keeper-begun` the route rests on the very fold it read (it never supersedes), naming its license (`licensed`: the branch, the revision, its tree, the recheck, the label, the CI run). The lei's return spends where the rite already spends it: the vest rides the live rite whose begin claims the kukulu lei's return for each lent face, so the keeper commit takes no extra return, and `keeper-vested` names each return (`leis`). A licensed commit whose vest meets no live rite, whose rite's vouches name another branch, head or tree than its license, or that meets the floor off, lapses and hands nothing. The license reads the vessel's faces and nothing past them (operator ruling, 2026-11-24): a standing wide kukulu lei on the branch among the vessel's lent faces, strung by the hand's route, licenses the vest for every face the vessel holds, and a face whose begin reads absent spends nothing; a vessel opens only its own faces and relays every other document. `lares pulama status` names each licensed commit (`keeperLei.licensed`: its link, branch, revision, CI run and, once vested, the lei returns). The vest, the receive, the land and the take-fire run unchanged, and the struck-light floor stands as before. A face of two devices refuses `keeper-lei-cosign-owed` and names the routed `keeper-commit`, which asks the co-sign. The floor is a code constant beside `KEEPER_STRUCK_EVERY` (`KEEPER_LEI_STANDS`), inside the closure, never an env var or a tiddler: turning it off moves `D′`, so the OFF switch lands through one keeper handoff, and `status` reads `floor: false`. ''The residual, named:'' every bound above binds honest successor code alone, and the host testifies the revision, the CI run and the label; a host can send the licensed commit itself, so the real gate stands at host access plus a standing kukulu lei, never at the merge gate. A signed CI attestation the keeper reads, and a second party on pūlama-closure diffs, stay the beta levers. <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/keeper-lei#/bounds`>>
* ''What the commit does not consent to.'' The closure names the pūlama's code alone, never the vessel image, so a new image still enters on an admission or a kukulu offer, as before.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/after-the-merge`>>

!! After the merge, in order

# ''The author and family condition'': the test author, the cure author and the cold reader come from distinct seats and model families, read from vessel-signed `roster/<seat>` records, never from commit co-author lines.
# ''The sampled re-run'' (Beer's System 3*): the vessel re-runs one randomly chosen check inside its own image before it co-signs, two diverse runners agreeing the way Wheeler's diverse double-compiling defeats a poisoned compiler; no receipt stands beneath a return today, so the remote's CI carries the test verdict until this slice lands.
# ''The in-place ingest'' (#/the-vm-tension).
# ''The keeper lei's beta road'' (#/the-keeper-tally): a per-lei opt-in (`lares lei kui … --keeper`, the keeper an affordance of the kukulu lei behind the flag); a signed CI attestation the keeper reads in place of the host's testimony; one spend per rite in the tally rather than one per lent face; a ward verb `ward-keeper-lei` co-signs over the kukulu lei's stringing (its cid, the roster), each licensed commit carrying that one spent co-sign in place of a per-commit one, so a face of two devices hands the fire hands-off too; `lares lei show` names the keeper commits each kukulu lei return licensed (the vessel reads the vest's `leis` off the pūlama); and a second party at the merge on pūlama-closure diffs (the operator's own approve, or a CODEOWNERS rule over `tools/pulama-closure.mjs --list`), the one bind that holds past the familiar's merge gate.
# ''The browser's lend'' (road rank 4d): a browser vessel pairs into each face's PersonaGroup as a second device member by the human's act, its lend read off its group memberships, never off the node's pūlama; the browser holder routes each island message to the session it serves, and the PersonaGroup ring's own-hand path checks the grant; a tab reload re-stands the browser, its custody reopening by the human's gesture or a session-bound key held for the tab's life.

The rite itself writes a `rite-begun` receipt before the vessel goes down and the new vessel writes `rite-landed` naming it; an unmatched begun receipt reads red at the next wake, two halves of one tessera across a restart. Spent records retire outside the hearth in small batches: in 1834 the Exchequer burned its obsolete tally sticks in the stove beneath the House of Lords and burned down the Palace of Westminster. Burn the token, keep the receipt.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/records`>>

!! Records, not rows

Three different things answer to the word "row" in the design's talk-out; this carrier names each by what it holds:

* a ''signed record'' (`SignedSwarmAct` in code): a JSON `{body, signer, sig}` resting as a tiddler's text in the vessel's control document, under `$:/lares/swarm/…`: a lei, its return, a tessera half, a receipt, a roster reading, a vouch. The token family and its receipts live here.
* a ''stage meme'': a whole memetic carrier under the memory stage, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/<class>/<slug>`; the code keys it by its title's path beneath the stage, and a path that reads otherwise refuses `stage-path-shape`.
* a ''road item'': one line of the roadmap carrier's table, named by what it holds: a lane, a ruling, a stride's work.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open`>>

!! Open

* The code wears the names (the core `iris`, the Node binding's verb `lares mcp aho`, the lei, `kui`, the return, `unstring`, `restring`, the tessera, conferre). What still waits: iris on the 2026-07-28 MCP revision; the sampled re-run (no receipt stands beneath a return); the rite against a real container house.
* A kumu's read of pūlama, kūkulu and wanaʻao, and the family's read of the lei protocol.
* The fence (#/the-fence).
* Whether a push or keep lei ever binds a set of groups the way a lend binds its kūkulu leis (default: one group per lei), and the names the guard's refusals take in code.
* The cord's binding placement: the core in `@lararium/mesh` and the Node binding in `lares-cli` (default), against a binding in `@lararium/node`.
* Unverified: Claude Code's behaviour behind a cord that never exits, on the operator's installed version; the internals of reloaderoo and mcpmon (prior art for holding a client across a server respawn); whether the vessel key lives in process memory alone; TPM reach inside WSL2 and Docker.

